A||||8||pHoOl||Higher octane fuel||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||03-24-2001||12:42 PM||||Does anyone know what difference if any this makes?  I had asked my dad (engineer) and he had said that you should put in whatever fuel the car was designed for.  Something like if i put in premium, it wouldn't make it run any better, and would probably hurt performance.  Anybody have any answers?<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-24-2001||12:53 PM||||Octane rating shows how much heat (compression) the fuel will take before detonating (knock, ping)...<BR>So if you have an engine with 8.5 or 9:1 compression ratio, you can use regular pump gas.<BR>If you have 9.5 or 10:1 compression, then use premium gas.<BR>If you have over 10:1 compression, then you would want an additive to boost the octane ratio...<BR>Buying premium fuel for a car that can use regular only wastes money, but the premium also has more additives to clean the fuel system too...<BR>But it won't hurt performance, as the gas has the same BTU rating in regular and premium...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-24-2001||02:20 PM||||Actually, "premium" fuel often isn't any better from an aditive angle either.<P>There are a number of articles at <A HREF="http://www.motor.com" TARGET=_blank>www.motor.com</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.motorage.com" TARGET=_blank>www.motorage.com</A>  about cleaners fuels and injector fouling.<P>Most stock Fiero's should run fine on 87-89 octain fuel. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||03-25-2001||07:47 AM||||If your engine doesn't ping under load putting a higher octane gas in it would be a waste of money!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||03-27-2001||06:39 PM||||Actually high octane fuel has slightly lower btu per pound than lower octane fuel.  The octane booster additives account for this.||||reg||1
Z||000005||862M4inCA||03-27-2001||07:17 PM||||the higher the octane rating on fuel is relative to how hot and how high the pressure needs to be before combustion without the help of a spark will occur. For example if an engine running 87 octane fuel pings it's most likely (timing set aside here) going to be caused by high combustion temperatures, compression, or both. Running a higher octane fuel instead would most likely eleminate preignition. This is why most newer engine recommend higher octane fuels as they are designed to run hotter and typically have higher compression in their stock form than older engines did. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||03-27-2001||08:05 PM||||Higher octane fuel burns slower and thus needs timing adjusting (advance) to get any advantage. If your car runs ok with 87 oct (as designed originaly) then using higher octane without adjusting timing is a total waste of money and performance. That's why they say to just use the octane rating recommended for your car.<BR>I know so many people that thinks that just using 93 will give them performance, etc, or that is cleaner. Gas will be as clean as the station gas tanks are. Just use what your ignition timing will allow.||||reg||1
Z||000007||99BlackZ||03-27-2001||10:25 PM||||I read an article in a magazine that actually showed that higher octane fuel actually hurt performance.  (All else being equal)<BR>If your setup can take 87 then use it.<BR>This article took a supercharged 5.0 mustang and they were testing a number of variables.  One experiment they tried, was using 87 octane instead of the 92.  HP increased accross the board.  They said that they couldn't run it on the street, for fear of detonation with that setup, but it proved the point.<BR>You use the higher octane so you can bump timing or increase compression which increases hp.  The higher octane does not.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bill H||03-27-2001||11:34 PM||||I work in the service dept for a Dodge and Jeep dealership. Engineers state High octane will make some engines ping. Is this true who knows but this does solve pinging in alot of 8cyl chrysler engines.||||reg||1
